F. Noise: what a shire's contrast must beat
==============================================================================================================
camera, one frame per pixel (live view): NETD 24.5 / 29.4 / 34.6 mK
camera, difference image 1 s per state, shire ROI: 2.46 / 2.97 / 3.48 mK
camera, lock-in    60 s, per quadrature, shire ROI: 0.321 / 0.387 / 0.454 mK
camera, lock-in   600 s, per quadrature, shire ROI: 0.101 / 0.122 / 0.144 mK
camera, lock-in  3600 s, per quadrature, shire ROI: 0.0414 / 0.05 / 0.0586 mK
sensor raw codes, lock-in    60 s, per quadrature: 1.5 / 2.98 / 6.9 mK; whole degrees: 19.5 / 21.7 / 24.4 mK
sensor raw codes, lock-in   600 s, per quadrature: 0.474 / 0.944 / 2.18 mK; whole degrees: 6.17 / 6.86 / 7.71 mK
sensor raw codes, lock-in  3600 s, per quadrature: 0.193 / 0.385 / 0.891 mK; whole degrees: 2.52 / 2.8 / 3.15 mK
criterion: a shire is seen when its contrast is 3 times the noise of an ROI pair (sqrt 2 x one ROI); SNR 5 instead takes 2.8 times as long
camera thresholds, K x sqrt 2 x the ROI noise above, mK (median [5-95]): diff 1 s/state 13 [10-15]; lock-in 60 s 1.6 [1.4-1.9]; lock-in 600 s 0.52 [0.43-0.61]; lock-in 3600 s 0.21 [0.18-0.25]; the 1 s threshold applies to the settled contrast, the lock-in ones to the fundamental's amplitude, (2/pi) x the difference image's amplitude at f_mod (section D), which is a fraction of the settled contrast

